            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house had an excessive amount of air leakage, and there are some key areas that we identified. The majority of the air leakage is associated with those 2 heat duct openings into the unconditioned crawlspace. This situation not affects heat loss but also significantly increases air leakage from your home, and creates a path for poor air quality to enter the upper living spaces above. This really needs to be remedied !!



Other air leakage locations are associated with the 6" furnace chimney, the 6" open combustion air supply, the 6" non-dampered fresh air supply, the fireplace, and around the attic hatch (the hatch can be sealed with a removable caulking such as Draft Stop). If you complete the heating &amp; hot water system upgrades outlined within this report, you will significantly reduce the excessive air leakage from your home.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 20% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 194&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 600 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 construction with R20 batt insulation, including the wall adjoining the garage. In conjunction with a future exterior renovation of the house, I would recommend you add a minimum 2" (R10) of insulation, including the wall adjoining the garage.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an additional R10 insulation to 100% of the exterior walls of your home, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 241&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;15.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

CRAWLSPACE WALLS



Your crawlspace has an insulated floor above and the walls are uninsulated concrete. I have modelled adding a minimum R24 of rigid foam insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R24 of rigid foam insulation to the crawlspace walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 96&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area. The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;b&gt;The upgrade recommendation for your domestic hot water is integrated into the boiler upgrade recommendation above.&lt;/b&gt;</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Home Heating &amp; Hot Water + other Zone Heating&lt;/b&gt;



HEATING



Your house is primarily heated with an older oil-fired furnace (venting up a 6" chimney) that would be lucky to still be functioning at about 83% Steady State Efficiency. This furnace has a 6" open combustion air supply pipe and a 6" fresh air supply connected to the return air plenum. The heat distribution ductwork for this furnace travels through an unconditioned crawlspace below, and a prior owner of this house has cut into the ductwork to provide some heat into this crawlspace. In my opinion, this should never have been done, and this ductwork now represents significant heat loss from your house (via air leakage) and a path for contaminated air from the crawlspace to enter the interior living space of your home.



Your house also has a supplemental heating system via the older wood fireplace. It appears the damper on the fireplace seals well, and there is a combustion air supply from the outside however the glass doors are definitely not air tight, so this fireplace will also draw inside air for combustion. Of greater issue is when the fire is dying down and the damper cannot yet be closed. Many folks will just leave the damper open for the evening, or perhaps forget about it and the chimney will remain open for days or weeks .......... continuously sucking indoor warm air up and out.



The garage is heated with an oil space heater, however the heating requirements of the garage are outside the calculations of this home energy evaluation. Similarly, the partial heating of the unconditioned crawlspace (that should never have been done) is also excluded from this evaluation. What is included is the excessive air leakage from your home that results from those 2 openings into the crawlspace.



You have a very large electric heat load requirement from the hot tub in the back yard, and this energy use is also excluded from the energy evaluation. The Home Energy Report does include an estimate of the energy use (in GJ) for a heated garage and a hot tub. From some electricity monitoring you have completed, you estimate the hot tub increases your electricity bill by $150 - $200 per month



HOT WATER



Your house has a 178 litre electric hot water tank, and although electricity in Yellowknife(primarily a hydro generation community) is relatively GHG free, having an electric hot water tank can be very expensive.



AN INTEGRATION OPPORTUNITY



During the assessment we talked about an opportunity for you to install an ENERGY STAR® direct vent, condensing propane boiler (95% AFUE) that could be zoned to accomplish many objectives:



1. Use one zone to heat the main floor &amp; second floor of the house via a fan coil with an integrated heat exchanger mounted where the current oil furnace exist. You could have the fan of this unit wired to circulate the warm air from the fireplace. The fresh air supply duct can still be used (with a damper to control the amount of fresh air) however the 6" open combustion air pipe can be eliminated.



2. With the tile ceiling on the main floor, you do have the option of installing some in-floor radiant heat to the upper level, using another thermostatically controlled zone.



3. Another zone can be used to heat your domestic hot water, still including an integrated water storage tank to prevent short cycling of the boiler. This tank can likely be located in where the current furnace is.



4. Another zone could be used (via a heat exchanger) to heat your hot tub.



5. You could even add a fancoil heat exchanger on another zone to heat your garage.



6. I do not recommend heating the crawlspace. Although I do see many houses with a loop from the boiler in the crawlspace &amp; set to 5 degrees ......... I think it is better for you to adequately insulate all exposed plumbing and make sure the heating system ductwork has all the joints sealed and then ensure a minimum R20 insulation is on all exposed surfaces.













&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

My savings calculations are based on items 1, 2 &amp; 3 above. By replacing your heating system with an ENERGY STAR® direct vent, condensing propane boiler (95% AFUE), and using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (with an integrated storage tank), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,263&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;38.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Woodstove&lt;/b&gt;



If you decide to install a woodstove insert to replace the fireplace, I recommend installing an EPA-certified wood-burning appliance. Ensure that the installation complies with local building, fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an EPA-certified woodstove and using it for 50% of your heat load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 845&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. Harvesting your own would will increase these savings.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil furnace does have a 6" open combustion air supply. If you keep this furnace, I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace are being satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the furnace is operating.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation. 











&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system near the front of the left-facing roof (orientation = SE), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 709&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year in a hydro community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood fan, 2 bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.8 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home from your combustion appliances (furnace and wood fireplace).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



